==English==
{{was wotd|2018|October|21}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|dum|tatelen}}, {{m|dum|tateren||to babble, chatter}}, originally {{glossary|imitative}}.<ref>{{R:Oxford Dictionaries Online}}</ref> The word is cognate with {{cog|nds|tateln}}, {{m|nds|täteln||to cackle, gabble}}.<ref>{{R:Oxford English Dictionary|part of speech=sb|volume=IX|part=2|page=111|column=2}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈtæt(ə)l/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/ˈtætl̩/|/-ɾl̩/|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|tat|tle|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|tattl}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[chatter#Verb|chatter]]; to [[gossip#Verb|gossip]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Much Ado About Nothing Q|act=II|scene=i|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=5Lo2AQAAMAAJ&pg=PT16|passage=He were an excellent man that were made iuſt in the mid-way between him and Benedick, the one is too like an image and ſaies nothing, and the other too like my ladies eldeſt ſonne, euermore '''tatling'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Juvenal Satires|chapter=Dedication|page=ix|passage=By this time, My Lord, I doubt not but that you wonder, why I have run off from my Biaſs ſo long together, and made ſo tedious a Digreſſion from Satire to Heroique Poetry. But if You will not excuſe it, by the '''tattling''' Quality of Age, which, as Sir ''{{w|William Davenant}}'' ſays, is always Narrative; yet I hope the uſefulneſs of what I have to ſay on this Subject, will qualifie the remoteneſs of it; {{...}}}}
# {{senseid|en|to report others' wrongdoings or violations}}{{lb|en|intransitive|Canada|US|pejorative}} ''Often said of children'': to [[report#Verb|report]] [[incriminating#Adjective|incriminating]] [[information]] about another person, or a person's [[wrongdoing]]; to [[tell on]] somebody. {{defdate|from late 15th c.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[speak]] like a [[baby#Noun|baby]] or [[young#Adjective|young]] [[child]]; to [[babble#Verb|babble]], to [[prattle]]; to speak [[haltingly]]; to [[stutter#Verb|stutter]].<!--
#* Attested in 1481 in William Caxton's translation of “[[w:Reynard|The History of Reynard the Fox]]”-->

